CARDS = [
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "What is the difference between a voluntary and a nonvoluntary context switch, and how would you use that?", "Voluntary means the process gave up the CPU because it had nothing to do — a blocking call. Nonvoluntary means the kernel took it away because the slice expired or something more urgent appeared. Both are in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code>. A healthy I/O-bound service is mostly voluntary; a rising nonvoluntary count means it wants more CPU than it is getting, which is exactly what cgroup throttling looks like from inside the process."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "How does epoll actually get told that a socket is ready?", "Registering a descriptor attaches a callback to that socket's wait queue — the same wait queue a blocking read would sleep on. When the network softirq finishes TCP processing and appends data to the socket's receive buffer, it walks that wait queue and the callback pushes the descriptor onto the epoll instance's ready list. So the list is maintained continuously by the events themselves, and <code>epoll_wait</code> does not scan anything."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "Explain the futex fast path.", "The lock is an ordinary integer in your process's memory. Acquiring is a compare-and-swap in user space — a few nanoseconds, no system call. Only if that fails does the thread call <code>futex(FUTEX_WAIT)</code>, which atomically rechecks the value and sleeps on a kernel queue keyed by that address. Releasing calls FUTEX_WAKE only if it can see a waiter. Since real locks are uncontended most of the time, locking is essentially free — and a high futex count in strace is direct evidence of genuine contention."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "What is false sharing?", "Two threads writing to two different variables that happen to occupy the same 64-byte cache line. Cache coherence works at line granularity, so the line bounces between cores on every write while the code remains perfectly correct. Order-of-magnitude slowdowns with nothing visible in a profile. The fix is padding, which is why high-performance data structures are full of alignment attributes."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "Why did KPTI cost so much performance?", "Kernel memory was mapped into every process's page table, marked supervisor-only, precisely so a system call did not need a page table switch. Meltdown made that mapping readable through a speculative side channel, so the mitigation unmaps it — and now every syscall reloads <code>cr3</code> and disturbs address translation. Syscall-heavy workloads lost five to thirty percent, which is why <code>io_uring</code> and other batching interfaces became more valuable."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "What does io_uring change architecturally?", "Two ring buffers shared between the process and the kernel: you write submission entries and read completions, so many operations cost one crossing — or none at all in polled mode. More importantly it is completion-based rather than readiness-based, which is what makes it work for regular files, where readiness is meaningless. It is the first Linux interface that lets a single-threaded event loop do genuinely asynchronous file I/O without a thread pool."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "How does eBPF run user-supplied code in the kernel safely?", "A verifier statically proves, before loading, that the program terminates — no unbounded loops — that every memory access is in bounds, and that it only calls permitted helpers. Then it is JIT-compiled to native code. It is a proof obligation substituting for a hardware boundary, and it is why modern observability, container networking and runtime security are all built on it."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "How does a debugger set a breakpoint?", "It overwrites the first byte of the target instruction with 0xCC — the int3 opcode — saving the original. Execution raises an exception, which becomes SIGTRAP, which suspends the process and notifies the debugger through ptrace. To continue it restores the byte, single-steps one instruction, re-inserts the breakpoint, and resumes. A debugger edits your machine code while it runs."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "Why does V8 use pointer compression and what does it cap?", "Object references in the heap are stored as 32-bit offsets from a base rather than 64-bit pointers. Half the memory for pointers, and — more importantly — twice as many pointers per cache line, so pointer-chasing through the heap misses cache half as often. The cost is that a single V8 heap is capped at 4 GB of compressed address space, which is why <code>--max-old-space-size</code> above about 4096 does nothing and the answer to 'give Node 16 GB' is 'run four processes'."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "What is PSI and why is it better than utilisation?", "Pressure Stall Information reports the fraction of time tasks were stalled waiting for CPU, memory or I/O rather than how busy the resource was. Utilisation tells you the resource is working; pressure tells you your work is being delayed, which is what you actually care about. It is what modern autoscaling and out-of-memory daemons use, and it is per-cgroup, so it works per container."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "How do you profile a Node process when no JavaScript function accounts for the CPU?", "Drop a layer. <code>perf record</code> on the process, with <code>--perf-basic-prof</code> so V8 writes a map file letting perf resolve JIT frames — otherwise they show as bare addresses because the code was generated at run time with no symbol table. That is what shows you time in GC, in JSON serialisation, in TLS, in a native addon, or in the kernel, none of which the JavaScript profiler can see."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "What is descriptor passing and where does Node use it?", "A Unix domain socket can transfer an open file descriptor to another process using SCM_RIGHTS, where it lands as a new number pointing at the same kernel object. Node's cluster module uses it: the primary creates the listening socket and passes it to workers, which is how several processes accept on one port without any of them binding it. systemd socket activation uses the same mechanism."),
("plumbing", "Senior / internals", "Why is a thread switch cheaper than a process switch — precisely?", "One step is skipped: loading the page table root into <code>cr3</code>. Because threads share an address space, the TLB is not disturbed and the caches likely still hold shared data. Everything else — saving and restoring registers, the scheduler decision, the kernel stack switch — is identical. That single instruction and its cache consequences are the entire difference."),
]
